  "query_preview": ".. \nThe main focus continues to be on Typhoon Champi, which continues to\nstrengthen. Champi began Rapid Intensification earlier this morning,\nand appears like it could be going through an Eyewall Replacement \nCycle, as there's been a couple of expansions and contractions where \nthe eye's Central Dense Overcast (CDO), allowing the eye to become \nmore opaque. This has been preceded by many lightning bursts that \nhave mainly been confined to the southern flank of the eyewall, as \none would expect.\n\nGuam:\nThe Typhoon Warning remains in effect. Maximum sustained winds have\nincreased to 100 mph near the center, and it's forecast to continue\nstrengthening, with sustained winds of 115 mph probable late this\nevening. At this time, Champi looks to pass over the southern tip of\nGuam or just south between 4 AM and 6 AM, which will allow the storm\nto continue to intensify as it moves through. The worst conditions\nlook to occur over the southern half of the island, where peak\nsustained winds of 110 to 130 mph, with gusts to 160 mph, will be\npossible. Winds will increase this evening, becoming strong tropical\nstorm force. Typhoon force winds are expected early Sunday morning.\nThese winds will continue to veer to the southwest, decreasing\nthrough the afternoon. Damaging winds will continue into Sunday\nnight. \n\nSignificant coastal flooding of up to 7 feet is expected, with \nlandslides possibly causing road closures, especially south of Cross\nIsland Road.\n\nRota:\nSustained tropical storm force winds will move into the area this\nevening, becoming strong tropical storm force by morning. However, \nif the typhoon \"wobbles\" far enough north, typhoon winds would \npotentially occur. As such, the Typhoon Warning remains in effect.\n\nTinian and Saipan:\nSustained tropical storm force winds are anticipated later tonight. \nThese winds will develop after 7 PM, peaking between 3 AM and 9 Am \nwhen they become east- southeasterly. During this time, sustained \nwiths of 40 to 50 mph, with gusts to 70 mph.\n\nFor more information, please reference our Zone Forecast package and\nTyphoon Local Statement. \n\nAs TY Champi departs the region, a dry pattern looks to move in, with\nPOPs (Probability of Precipitation) dropping to around 20 percent and\nstaying at these levels at least into next weekend.",
